WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.000
When you build for the web with React, you already have a massive system of gears turning in your head.

2
00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:11.000
You know how to manage state, you understand hooks, and you think in modular components.

3
00:00:11.000 --> 00:00:16.000
But opening a React Native project for the first time feels disorienting.

4
00:00:16.000 --> 00:00:22.000
The DOM is gone, there are no HTML tags, and you won't find a single CSS file in the project.

5
00:00:22.000 --> 00:00:25.000
React Native functions like a new dialect.

6
00:00:25.000 --> 00:00:31.000
Since you already speak the language fluently, you just need to pick up the local vocabulary used for mobile devices.

7
00:00:31.000 --> 00:00:36.000
The React logic, the brain that handles your data, is identical to what you use on the web.

8
00:00:36.000 --> 00:00:41.000
We are simply swapping out the rendering engine or the body to talk to phone hardware.

9
00:00:41.000 --> 00:00:45.000
Because you understand how to structure applications and manage data flow,

10
00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:51.000
your transition to mobile is a straightforward process of mapping those skills to a new set of components.

11
00:00:51.000 --> 00:00:54.000
On the web, code lives inside a browser.

12
00:00:54.000 --> 00:01:01.000
On a mobile phone, that middleman is removed, allowing your code to connect directly to the native interface.

13
00:01:01.000 --> 00:01:06.000
Without a browser to interpret them, standard HTML tags no longer work.

14
00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:10.000
We replace them with native UI instructions the phone can understand.

15
00:01:10.000 --> 00:01:13.000
This map shows the direct translations.

16
00:01:13.000 --> 00:01:19.000
A div becomes a view, and a P or span tags are replaced by text components.

17
00:01:19.000 --> 00:01:23.000
Even though the names change, the tree structure remains the same.

18
00:01:23.000 --> 00:01:27.000
You still nest components exactly as you would in a web layout.

19
00:01:27.000 --> 00:01:29.000
Styling follows a similar shift.

20
00:01:29.000 --> 00:01:36.000
Instead of external CSS files, we use JavaScript objects to define how our elements look and feel.

21
00:01:36.000 --> 00:01:40.000
One major change is the lack of CSS inheritance.

22
00:01:40.000 --> 00:01:44.000
On the web, applying color to a container changes text inside too.

23
00:01:44.000 --> 00:01:47.000
In React Native, the text remains unaffected.

24
00:01:47.000 --> 00:01:53.000
You must apply properties directly to the text component itself, rather than relying on a parent container.

25
00:01:53.000 --> 00:01:56.000
You are still using familiar logic to build your UI.

26
00:01:56.000 --> 00:02:03.000
You're just applying specific localized instructions to each piece, instead of relying on a global cascade.

27
00:02:03.000 --> 00:02:06.000
Moving between screens also changes.

28
00:02:06.000 --> 00:02:11.000
Mobile apps don't use URLs, so traditional routing doesn't apply here.

29
00:02:11.000 --> 00:02:14.000
Mobile uses a stack model.

30
00:02:14.000 --> 00:02:19.000
Navigating to a new area is like pushing a new screen onto the top of a deck.

31
00:02:19.000 --> 00:02:25.000
Hitting the back button pops that screen off the stack, revealing the ones sitting directly underneath it.

32
00:02:25.000 --> 00:02:28.000
User interactions change from clicks to touches.

33
00:02:28.000 --> 00:02:34.000
Instead of an on-click event, you'll listen for an on-press event to handle user input.

34
00:02:34.000 --> 00:02:37.000
Rendering lists also requires a different approach.

35
00:02:37.000 --> 00:02:42.000
Mapping thousands of items at once can quickly exhaust a phone's limited memory.

36
00:02:42.000 --> 00:02:45.000
To handle long lists efficiently, you'll use flat list.

37
00:02:45.000 --> 00:02:52.000
This component only renders the items currently visible on the screen, which keeps the app fast and responsive.

38
00:02:52.000 --> 00:02:56.000
These tools are designed to work with the physical reality of a mobile device.

39
00:02:56.000 --> 00:03:00.000
Ensuring your app meets the performance expectations of mobile users.

40
00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:06.000
When you look at a web component and a native component side by side, the similarities are obvious.

41
00:03:06.000 --> 00:03:10.000
The top section, where your state and props live, is identical.

42
00:03:10.000 --> 00:03:13.000
Your hooks and data logic transfer perfectly.

43
00:03:13.000 --> 00:03:17.000
The code you wrote for the web is immediately useful in this new environment.

44
00:03:17.000 --> 00:03:20.000
You are using the same engine you've driven for years.

45
00:03:20.000 --> 00:03:24.000
You're just switching onto new tracks leading to the app store and play store.

46
00:03:24.000 --> 00:03:30.000
In the next lesson, we'll dive deeper into styling and explore how to build complex layouts without a DOM.

47
00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:33.000
Your background in web development is your greatest asset.

48
00:03:33.000 --> 00:03:37.000
Now, let's start applying those skills to the world of mobile apps.

